//Simitone's own UI strings.
//
//TS1's GameData/UIText.iff carries everything The Sims 1 itself displayed, and
//FreeSO's Content/UI/uitext/<lang>.dir/*.cst carries FreeSO's TSO additions.
//Neither has anywhere to put a string Simitone invented - the settings strip, the
//buy-mode search box, the rezone dialogs, the credits screen - so those were
//literals in .cs files and could not be translated at all. This table is that
//missing place: ContentStrings reads it in TS1 mode as well as TSO mode, under the
//table id taken from the file name, and SimitoneStrings.Get looks ids up here.
//
//File name -> table id: the text between the first and second "_", so this file is
//table f900. That is deliberately clear of FreeSO's f1xx tables and of TS1's
//numeric STR chunk ids, so it cannot shadow either. Renaming this file means
//renaming SimitoneStrings.Table, or every string silently falls back to English.
//
//Ids are stable. A translator may add ids (a new string in a later build), but
//renumbering or retiring one re-breaks every translation of it. Ids are grouped in
//blocks of ten by the file that used to hold the literal, so a new string goes
//next to its neighbours.
//
//Format, as ContentStrings.Load parses it: "id ^text^", one per line, "//" starts a
//comment line. A text may run over several lines but must not contain "^"; %s and
//%d are argument specifiers, substituted in order, and a specifier with no argument
//left has its "%" dropped. Every id below has an English fallback hardcoded at its
//call site, so a table that is missing, incomplete or untranslated shows English
//rather than blank labels or "***MISSING***".

//--- Credits panel (UICreditsPanel) ---
1 ^Credits^
2 ^Close^
3 ^Scroll with the mouse wheel, or click and drag the text.^

